Sand forms a conical pile whose radius always equals its height. Sand is delivered at $6$ m³/min. Find the rate at which the height increases when the height is $3$ m. Use $V=\frac13\pi r^2h$.

The diagram is a vertical cross section through the axis.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (-2,0)--(0,2)--(2,0)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (0,2)--(0,0);
\draw (0,0.18)--(0.18,0.18)--(0.18,0);
\path (0,1) node[left] {$h$};
\path (1,0) node[below] {$r$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

An inverted conical tank is $10$ m deep with a top radius of $4$ m. Water enters at $8$ m³/min. Find the rate at which the water depth increases when the depth is $5$ m. Use $V=\frac13\pi r^2h$.

The diagram is a vertical cross section through the axis; $r$ and $h$ describe the water.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (-1.6,4)--(0,0)--(1.6,4)--cycle;
\draw (-0.8,2)--(0.8,2);
\draw[dashed] (0,0)--(0,4);
\path (0,1) node[left] {$h$};
\path (0.4,2) node[above] {$r$};
\draw[<->] (2.1,0)--(2.1,4);
\path (2.1,2) node[right] {$10\ \mathrm{m}$};
\path (0.8,4) node[above] {$4\ \mathrm{m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A kite stays $24$ m above level ground and moves horizontally away from its holder at $3$ m/s. Find the rate at which the string is let out when the string is $40$ m long. Assume the string is straight and the holder is at ground level.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0)--(3.2,0)--(3.2,2.4)--cycle;
\draw (3,0)--(3,0.2)--(3.2,0.2);
\path (1.6,0) node[below] {$x$};
\path (3.2,1.2) node[right] {$24\ \mathrm{m}$};
\path (1.6,1.2) node[above left] {$s$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A ladder $15$ m long rests against a vertical wall. Its foot is $9$ m from the wall.
(a) Find the rate at which the top moves if the foot slides away at $0.2$ m/s.
(b) At the same position, find the rate at which the foot moves if the top slides down at $0.4$ cm/s.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,4.4)--(0,0)--(3.5,0);
\draw (0,4)--(3,0);
\draw (0,0.2)--(0.2,0.2)--(0.2,0);
\path (0,2) node[left] {$y$};
\path (1.5,0) node[below] {$x$};
\path (1.5,2) node[above right] {$15\ \mathrm{m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A person $1.8$ m tall walks away from a $9$ m streetlight at $1.6$ m/s. Find the rate at which the person's shadow lengthens.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,4)--(0,0)--(5,0)--cycle;
\draw (4,0)--(4,0.8);
\path (0,2) node[left] {$9\ \mathrm{m}$};
\path (4,0.4) node[left] {$1.8\ \mathrm{m}$};
\draw[<->] (0,-0.5)--(4,-0.5);
\path (2,-0.5) node[below] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (4,-0.5)--(5,-0.5);
\path (4.5,-0.5) node[below] {$s$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A trough is $6$ m long. Its cross section is a right isosceles triangle with the right angle at the bottom and the top horizontal. Find the inflow needed to make the water depth rise at $0.05$ cm/min when the depth is $40$ cm. Give your answer in litres per minute; $1$ L $=1000$ cm³.

The diagram shows the triangular cross section of the water.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (-2,2)--(0,0)--(2,2)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (0,0)--(0,2);
\draw (-0.16,0.16)--(0,0.32)--(0.16,0.16);
\path (0,1) node[right] {$h$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Use centimetres throughout, so the trough is $600$ cm long. The altitude bisects the right angle; each half is a $45^\circ$ right triangle, so the water-surface width is $2h$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (-2,2)--(0,0)--(2,2)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (0,0)--(0,2);
\draw (-0.16,0.16)--(0,0.32)--(0.16,0.16);
\path (0,1) node[right] {$h$};
\path (-1,2) node[above] {$h$};
\path (1,2) node[above] {$h$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{aligned} V&=\frac12(2h)h(600)\\ &=600h^2\\ \frac{dV}{dh}&=1200h\\ \frac{dV}{dt}&=\frac{dV}{dh}\times\frac{dh}{dt}\\ &=1200h(0.05) \end{aligned}$

At $h=40$:
$\begin{aligned} \frac{dV}{dt}&=1200(40)(0.05)\\ &=2400\ \text{cm}^3/\text{min}\\ &=2.4\ \text{L}/\text{min} \end{aligned}$
